<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1856 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The anthem of Wales, written in 1856 by Evan James and his son James James; in 1905 a rugby crowd sang it before a match.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Evan James</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>James James</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Welsh</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Pontypridd, Glamorgan, Wales</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>January 1856</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Evan James, music by his son James James, January 1856)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hen-wlad-fy-nhadau">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</a>&quot; (&quot;The Old Land of My Fathers&quot;, usually rendered &quot;Land of My Fathers&quot;) was written in January 1856 by Evan James, with a tune by his son James James, both of Pontypridd in Glamorgan; the earliest written copy survives in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Known at first as &quot;Glan Rhondda&quot; (&quot;Banks of the Rhondda&quot;), it was first sung by Elizabeth John of Pontypridd in the vestry of the original Capel Tabor in Maesteg in January or February 1856, and it soon became popular in the district. James James was a harpist who played in the public house he ran, for dancing; the song was first meant to go in 6/8 time and had to be slowed to its present 3/4 when large crowds began to sing it.</p><p>Its fame grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a> Eisteddfod of 1858, where Thomas Llewelyn of Aberdare won a competition for an unpublished collection of Welsh airs with a collection that included &quot;Glan Rhondda&quot;. The adjudicator, John Owen (&quot;Owain Alaw&quot;, 1821–1883), asked to include it in his Gems of Welsh Melody (1860–1864), which gave it the title &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hen-wlad-fy-nhadau">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</a>&quot; and sold in large numbers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. At the Bangor Eisteddfod of 1874 it was sung by Robert Rees (&quot;Eos Morlais&quot;), one of the leading Welsh soloists of the day; it was sung more and more at patriotic gatherings and gradually became a national anthem. On 11 March 1899 Madge Breese recorded it for the Gramophone Company, in the first recording made in the Welsh language.</p><p>It was the first national anthem sung at the start of a sporting event. In 1905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s first touring rugby team, who began every match with a haka; in reply, the Wales player Teddy Morgan led the crowd in singing the anthem. For the next decades it was sung with &quot;God Bless the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>&quot; before matches, until sports officials decided in 1975 that it should be sung alone. Like the other British anthems it has no status in law, but since the 1970s it has been used at official ceremonies, including the opening of the Welsh Parliament (Senedd Cymru), and at receptions of the British monarch; a petition to make it the official anthem was debated in 2014 and judged &quot;not currently a possible development&quot;. Usually only the first stanza and the chorus are sung, in Welsh, and &quot;God Save the King&quot; is sometimes played beside it at events with a royal connection.</p><p>Not knowing the words has been held against politicians: in 1993 the new Secretary of State for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, John Redwood, was filmed failing to mime them, and according to John Major's autobiography his successor William Hague at once looked for someone to teach him; he found Ffion Jenkins, whom he later married. In 1978 Geraint Jarman a'r Cynganeddwyr recorded a version with electric guitars, played by Tich Gwilym and inspired by Jimi Hendrix's &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a>&quot;. The words &quot;Pleidiol wyf i'm gwlad&quot; (&quot;I am devoted to my country&quot;), from the refrain, were inscribed on the edge of the Welsh £1 coins of 1985, 1990, 1995 and 2000 and form the motto of the Royal Badge of Wales adopted in 2008. The tune was adapted for the anthems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> (&quot;Bro Goth agan Tasow&quot;) and Brittany (&quot;Bro Gozh ma Zadoù&quot;), and for &quot;Gwlad Newydd y Cymry&quot; (&quot;The New Country of the Welsh&quot;), written by Lewis Evans, who emigrated to Y Wladfa, the Welsh-speaking settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of love for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, the old land of the singer's fathers: it honours the country's poets and singers and the patriots who gave their lives for it, praises its mountains and streams, and in the chorus pledges devotion to the land and the hope that its ancient language will endure. The mood is proud and tender.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Welsh</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Mae hen wlad fy nhadau yn annwyl i mi,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Gwlad beirdd a chantorion, enwogion o fri;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Ei gwrol ryfelwyr, gwladgarwyr tra mad,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Dros ryddid collasant eu gwaed.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Gwlad, Gwlad, pleidiol wyf i'm gwlad,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Tra môr yn fur i'r bur hoff bau,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">O bydded i'r heniaith barhau.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Hen Gymru fynyddig, paradwys y bardd,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Pob dyffryn, pob clogwyn, i'm golwg sydd hardd;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Trwy deimlad gwladgarol, mor swynol yw si</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Ei nentydd, afonydd, i fi.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Os treisiodd y gelyn fy ngwlad dan ei droed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Mae heniaith y Cymry mor fyw ag erioed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Ni luddiwyd yr awen gan erchyll law brad,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Na thelyn berseiniol fy ngwlad.</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>O land of my fathers, O land of my love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Dear mother of minstrels who kindle and move,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And hero on hero, who at honour's proud call,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>For freedom their lifeblood let fall.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wales! Wales! O but my heart is with you!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And long as the sea</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your bulwark shall be,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To Cymru my heart shall be true.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>O land of the mountains, the bard's paradise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whose precipice proud, valleys lone as the skies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Green murmuring forest, far echoing flood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fire the fancy and quicken the blood.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>For tho' the fierce foeman has ravaged your realm,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The old speech of Cymru he cannot o'erwhelm,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our passionate poets to silence command</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Or banish the harp from your strand.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Evan James (1856). Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://cy.wikisource.org/wiki/Hen_wlad_fy_nhadau" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hen wlad fy nhadau</a>); public domain.</p><p>English: tr. Alfred Percival Graves (1905). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Hen_Wlad_Fy_Nhadau_(Graves)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
